# Authenticated privacy consumer — v13 preview

This opt-in consumer adds `/privacy` preferences, `/privacy/personalization`
and `/privacy/export`. It supports the recognized SaaS or full LMS starter and
composes with `make:age-gate` in either installation order. Custom authentication,
unknown privacy dependencies, ambiguous routes and existing output files need
manual review. Until publication admission, the dependency uses the matching
unpublished source path supplied explicitly to the generator.

## Configure and initialize

Provision `RULLST_PRIVACY_FORM_KEY_HEX` as an independent random 32-byte secret
encoded as 64 hexadecimal characters, shared only by trusted app processes.
Do not reuse a session, age or provider key. Rotating this secret invalidates
open forms; it does not change recorded choices. Never commit real secrets.

Set `RULLST_PRIVACY_DATABASE` to a new file in an existing private local directory.
All app processes must use that same file. Initialize it once from the project,
with the variable exported into the process environment:

```sh
cargo run --bin privacy-init
```

Initialization refuses existing files; application startup never repairs or
recreates consent state. A failed/cancelled bootstrap may leave a partial file
for explicit operator inspection. Ordinary `cargo run` still selects the main
application binary. The generated store has a 10,000 subject/tenant/purpose quota;
plan capacity explicitly before deployment. Expired choices and withdrawals are
not evicted automatically. This shared-local SQLite profile is not multi-host
replication and must not use a network filesystem.

Preferences and personalization fail closed when their state or form key is
unavailable. The authenticated account export does not depend on either one.
Each operation has a bounded wait. Keep the starter's authentication, CSRF,
security headers and production Server baseline, and deploy behind HTTPS.

## Meaning of the choice

The only purpose installed here is `personalized-greeting`: allowing this page
to use the account name for its optional greeting. It defaults to generic text.
This is not email marketing, analytics or permission for another processing
purpose. The operator selects the displayed notice version and grant lifetime
explicitly; the engineering lifetime cap is 365 days, not a legal retention rule.

The form binds its notice/version, revision, authenticated account, tenant and
session with an expiring HMAC proof. A switched account/session, changed school,
tampered or expired form cannot apply an old choice. The LMS school selector is
only a hint; current active membership is checked again before every request.
Use `/privacy?school=...` when membership requires explicit selection. Form and
download links retain the school selection for ordinary browser navigation.

Refusal/withdrawal makes the next greeting generic. A stale positive form cannot
undo a completed withdrawal, and a new notice requires a new explicit choice.
The check reads authoritative state before this optional action. Any deferred
integration you add must check again when its job executes. In-flight external
effects are not cancelled retroactively; stronger domain atomicity needs an
application transaction/processor contract. Authentication itself remains a
separate processing purpose and uses the starter's existing account lookup.

## Account-profile export

The concrete SQL adapter projects only the authenticated account's ID, name and
email. It has no caller-selected account ID and never returns credential fields.
Fields and JSON output are bounded. The response is a private JSON attachment,
with no-store/no-referrer headers; no public or retained export file is created.
The framework cannot remove a file after the user downloads it.

This is an **account-profile-only** export. It does not export learning progress,
subscriptions, billing, messages, guardian records, backups or processor copies,
and it never marks an existing queued privacy request as fulfilled. Add explicit
authorized adapters for those scopes before claiming a broader rights workflow.

## Operations and remaining responsibilities

Add a clearly visible link to preferences and the export in your application
navigation. Complete the operator's data inventory, lawful-basis assessment,
notices, retention/hold policy, broader rights adapters and support channel.
This consumer does not certify automatic worldwide compliance or age/guardianship.

Scope digests in the consent store are pseudonymous personal data. The operator
owns file permissions, synchronized clocks, durable storage and backup policy.
Before restoring older state, stop optional processing, reconcile withdrawals
and move all active purposes to fresh notice versions; old grants must not
silently resume. Never reuse retired versions. This consumer does not claim
automatic backup rollback detection or erasure of historical copies.
